  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "$\\frac{5}{6+2\\text{i}}=$",
  "worked_solution": "The denominator is $6+2\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $6-2\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $6-2\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6+2\\text{i}} & =\\frac{5}{6+2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{6-2\\text{i}}{6-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-10\\text{i}}{36-12\\text{i}+12\\text{i}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-10\\text{i}}{36-4{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-10\\text{i}}{36-4(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-10\\text{i}}{36+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-10\\text{i}}{40}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{30-10\\text{i}}{40}=\\frac{30}{40}-\\frac{10}{40}\\text{i}=\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
